# .dev-stack.env.example —— give THIS checkout its own dev stack.
#
# Copy to .dev-stack.env (git-ignored) in any checkout that needs to run `make dev-up` /
# `make test` at the same time as another one. Without it nothing changes: every value
# below has the default that used to be hardcoded, so the primary checkout needs no file.
#
# Not `.env.local`: env-lint pairs the root `.env.example` with that name and requires the key
# sets to match, and `.env.example` is the production compose contract. These are dev-stack
# knobs and have no business in it.
#
# Why it exists: docker-compose.dev.yml names the project `standmeet-dev` and publishes
# fixed host ports, so two checkouts on one machine drive the SAME containers. The second
# `make dev-up` recreates the first one's services out from under it, and what the first
# run then reports is not "someone restarted my stack" — it is a container-name conflict,
# a vanished container id, or a test that fails on data that was true a second ago.
#
# Pick numbers no other checkout uses. There is no allocator: N worktrees means N people
# choosing N sets, and the only rule is that they differ.
#
# Ports only. Every URL the e2e suite dials — BASE_URL, BACKEND_URL, the mock URLs, twelve of
# them — is DERIVED from these by the Makefile. Restating them here would be two lists to keep
# in agreement, and when they drift the symptom is a 401 or a refused connection against
# another checkout's stack, which reads as a broken test rather than a stale file.
#
# Unlike .env.example next door, this file keeps its values — they are the defaults, and a
# file whose whole job is "here is what to change" is unreadable with them stripped out.

# The compose project name. This is what separates one stack from another: containers,
# networks and volumes are all namespaced by it. Change it FIRST — two stacks sharing a
# project name will fight over container names no matter what the ports say.
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=standmeet-dev

# Published host ports. The container-side ports never change; only the host side, so
# nothing inside the stack is affected — services still talk to each other by name.
DEV_PORT_APP=38127
DEV_PORT_BACKEND=8000
DEV_PORT_DB=5432
DEV_PORT_REDIS=6379
DEV_PORT_MINIO=9200
DEV_PORT_MINIO_CONSOLE=9201
DEV_PORT_MAILPIT=18025

# Mocks. A parallel stack needs its own of these too: they are real listeners, and two
# stacks pointing at one mock means one run's assertions see the other run's traffic.
DEV_PORT_MCP_MOCK=9100
DEV_PORT_EXTERNAL_MOCK=9000
DEV_PORT_EXTERNAL_MOCK_TLS=9443
DEV_PORT_LLM_GATEWAY=9300
DEV_PORT_MAIL_MOCK=19400
